    "evidence_text": "DNA Deamination Mediates Innate Immunity to Retroviral Infection\n\nFurthermore, HIV Vif can protect MLV from this CEM15/APOBEC3G-dependent restriction.\n\nN348I in HIV-1 reverse transcriptase can counteract the nevirapine-mediated bias toward RNase H cleavage during plus-strand initiation.\n\nEfavirenz, a tight binding inhibitor, restricts the influence of the mutation.\n\nThe cytidine deaminase CEM15 induces hypermutation in newly synthesized HIV-1 DNA\n\nHigh mutation frequency during reverse transcription has a principal role in the genetic variation of primate lentiviral populations.\n\nIt is the main driving force for the generation of drug resistance and the escape from immune surveillance.\n\nThis effect can be counteracted by the HIV-1 virion infectivity factor (Vif).\n\nBroad antiretroviral defence by human APOBEC3G through lethal editing of nascent reverse transcripts\n\nWhen produced in the presence of APOBEC3G, vif-defective virus is non-infectious.\n\nActivating mutations in the NT5C2 nucleotidase gene drive chemotherapy resistance in relapsed ALL\n\nA novel molecular mechanism of dual resistance to nucleoside and nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors.\n\nN348I in the Connection Domain of HIV-1 Reverse Transcriptase Confers Zidovudine and Nevirapine Resistance\n\nAlmost all known RT inhibitor resistance mutations identified to date map to the polymerase domain of the enzyme.\n\nThe objective of this multidisciplinary study was to establish the in vivo relevance of this mutation and its role in drug resistance.\n\nHowever, this analysis did not account for the simultaneous selection of other RT or protease inhibitor resistance mutations on viral load.\n\nTo delineate the role of this mutation in RT inhibitor resistance, N348I was introduced into HIV-1 molecular clones containing different genetic backbones.\n\nN348I decreased zidovudine susceptibility 2- to 4-fold in the context of wildtype HIV-1 or when combined with TAMs.\n\nConclusions\n\nNonproductive human immunodeficiency virus type 1 infection in nucleoside-treated G0 lymphocytes.\n\nInfection of quiescent peripheral CD4 lymphocytes by HIV-1 results in incomplete, labile, reverse transcripts.\n\nHowever, the mechanism(s) involved in the blockage of reverse transcription remains undefined.\n\nIn this study we investigated whether nucleotide levels influence viral reverse transcription in G0 cells.\n\nThe human immunodeficiency virus type 1 nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor resistance mutation I132M confers hypersensitivity to nucleoside analogs.\n\nIn this study, we have further characterized the role of this mutation in viral replication capacity and in resistance to other RT inhibitors.\n\nFurthermore, the replication capacity of HIV-1 containing I132M is severely impaired.\n\nThis decrease in viral replication capacity could be partially or completely compensated for by the A62V or L214I mutation, respectively.\n\nResponses to interferon alpha (IFN-alpha) and zidovudine (AZT) have been documented but not with long-term follow-up.\n\nWe treated 15 ATLL patients with IFN and AZT.\n\nHypercalcaemia predicted a poor outcome but differences were not significant.\n\nEight of the 15 patients have died 3-41 months from diagnosis.\n\nIn conclusion, IFN and AZT improves the outcome of ATLL patients and helps maintain responses.",
